Detecting wildfires in real-time is challenging due to the significant differences between pre- and post-fire infrared and visible light images, particularly in how landforms and vegetation appear to the human eye. To address this, we introduce a deep learning-based approach that automatically learns a fusion mapping from paired visible and infrared images. This method incorporates specific constraints to enhance the model's ability to adapt to various landforms and improve the interpretability of the fused output for human observers. Evaluated on the CO2RI dataset, our approach achieves a mean Intersection over Union (IoU) of 0.83 with U-Net and 0.80 with ResNet18, outperforming existing state-of-the-art techniques. The source code is accessible at https://github.com/ChenYi-ASU/FIRe-GAN. Furthermore, an interactive demonstration is available here: https://chenyi-chen.github.io/Wildfire_Fusion_Demo/. We hope this work will be beneficial to the research community. Our paper is scheduled to be published in IEEE Transactions on Image Processing. If you are interested in image fusion or remote sensing, please feel free to contact the corresponding author at chen.yi2@asu.edu. We would be grateful for your stars!

