We investigate a charged particle moving through the vacuum radiation field, incorporating self-interaction via the Abraham-Lorentz force, alongside a charge within a resistive material medium that induces external radiation due to finite conductivity. The study examines the particle's trajectory, specifically analyzing exponential decay modes characteristic of electron motion in high-intensity laser fields. The resistive nature of the medium is demonstrated by showing the absence of an anomalous $g$-factor and super-critical acceleration; consequently, self-field-induced oscillations do not occur, and run-away solutions are precluded. Furthermore, we establish the existence of a stable critical acceleration above which the system exhibits exponential decay. The decay rate remains independent of the laser field's frequency but varies with its intensity and the medium's resistivity. These theoretical findings align with recent experimental results and complement earlier simulations. Finally, we explore the implications of extending the analysis to non-linear conductive media, such as Kerr materials. Keywords: radiation reaction, resistive medium, charge trajectory, charge decay, charge acceleration, charge oscillation, charge runaway, critical acceleration, nonlinear conductivity. PACS: 52.35.-g, 52.65.+y, 52.65.S-
